# Line endings — the single highest-impact Windows setting in this repo.
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true by default. Without the rules below, EVERY one of
# this repo's ~196 shell scripts is checked out with CRLF, and bash then fails on the very first
# function definition:
#
#   ./zuvo-collector-host.sh: line 21: syntax error near unexpected token `{'
#   bash: zuvo_collector_host: command not found
#
# (Demonstrated on this repo's own file before writing this.) The failure is not confined to
# scripts a developer runs — skills call ~/.zuvo/append-runlog 73 times at runtime, and every git
# hook is a shell script, so a CRLF checkout silently disables the quality gates too.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` normalises everything Git detects as text to LF in the working tree,
# regardless of the user's core.autocrlf. Binary files are matched explicitly below so Git never
# tries to translate them.

* text=auto eol=lf

# --- Executables and anything a shell/interpreter reads -----------------------
# Listed explicitly rather than relying on text=auto: a file Git heuristically classifies as
# binary (e.g. one containing an unlucky byte) would otherwise skip normalisation and ship CRLF.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
*.bats          text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.cjs           text eol=lf

# Extensionless helpers (scripts/zuvo-home/append-runlog, retro-stub, backlog, …) and hook
# entry points. These have shebangs and are executed directly.
scripts/zuvo-home/*     text eol=lf
hooks/*                 text eol=lf
bin/*                   text eol=lf
.githooks/*             text eol=lf

# --- Data and docs ------------------------------------------------------------
*.md            text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
*.tsv           text eol=lf
*.txt           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf

# --- Batch files: CRLF, EXCEPT the polyglot ----------------------------------
# cmd.exe mis-parses LF-only batch in multi-line parenthesised blocks, so plain batch keeps CRLF.
#
# hooks/run-hook.cmd is the exception and MUST stay LF. It is a bash/batch polyglot that every
# Claude Code hook routes through, so bash on macOS/Linux reads it on every hook invocation — and
# bash does NOT tolerate CRLF there. Verified before writing this: a CRLF copy produces
# "line 37: : command not found" and fails to resolve the hook. An earlier draft of this file
# pinned *.cmd to CRLF and would have broken every hook on macOS. The batch section inside
# run-hook.cmd is written with single-line conditionals precisely so LF is safe for cmd.exe too.
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
hooks/run-hook.cmd  text eol=lf

# --- Binary: never translate --------------------------------------------------
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.jpeg          binary
*.gif           binary
*.ico           binary
*.pdf           binary
*.zip           binary
*.gz            binary
*.tgz           binary
*.db            binary
*.sqlite        binary
*.db-shm        binary
*.db-wal        binary

# --- Deliberately preserved CRLF test vector ----------------------------------
# A real CSV exported by a Windows tool has CRLF, and this fixture is the ONLY CRLF input the
# leads dedup path ever sees. Normalising it to LF keeps the test green (verified) — which is
# precisely why it must be pinned: the coverage would vanish silently, with nothing failing.
scripts/tests/fixtures/leads-dedup/existing.csv -text
